# Reflection Report — RetainIQ Pro

## Introduction

Developing RetainIQ Pro was one of the most valuable practical machine learning experiences I have completed. The project allowed me to move beyond simply training machine learning models and instead focus on the full machine learning lifecycle, including experiment tracking, model deployment, monitoring, explainability, and Dockerized infrastructure.

Before starting this project, my understanding of machine learning was mainly centered around model accuracy and training workflows. Through RetainIQ Pro, I learned that building a successful AI system involves much more than achieving high prediction performance. Production systems require reproducibility, deployment reliability, monitoring, version control, and business-oriented design.

The project also helped me understand how MLOps bridges the gap between data science experimentation and real-world deployment.

---

# Initial Challenges

At the beginning of the project, one of the biggest challenges was organizing the architecture of the system. There were many moving parts:

- Machine learning training scripts
- Hyperparameter tuning
- MLflow integration
- API deployment
- Frontend dashboard
- Docker containers
- Monitoring scripts
- Explainability workflows

Initially, it was difficult to understand how all these components should interact together in a production-style environment.

Another major challenge was Docker containerization. Before this project, I had limited experience with Docker Compose and multi-container applications. Understanding how frontend, backend, and MLflow services communicate through containers required significant experimentation and debugging.

I also faced challenges with MLflow Model Registry integration, especially when loading production models from the registry inside Docker containers. Debugging model paths and fallback loading mechanisms helped me understand production deployment issues more deeply.

